----------|-------|\n| **Slack** | Jan 2025 | $150.00 | $0.00 | Invoice exists but QBO shows $0 |\n| **Microsoft 365** | Dec 2025 | $99.99 | $0.00 | Invoice exists but QBO shows $0 |\n\n**Total Missing:** $249.99\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Partial Matches (Amount Discrepancies)**\n\n| Vendor | Period | Invoice Amount | QBO Amount | Variance | Analysis |\n|--------|--------|----------------|------------|----------|----------|\n| **AWS** | Feb 2025 | $1,262.32 | $412.50 | -$849.82 | QBO significantly understated |\n| **Google Cloud Platform** | Feb 2025 | $425.00 | $1,262.32 | +$837.32 | QBO significantly overstated |\n| **AWS** | Apr 2025 | $1,050.00 | $1,000.00 | -$50.00 | QBO slightly understated |\n\n**Net Variance:** -$62.50\n\n---\n\n### 💡 **Analysis & Likely Explanations**\n\n1. **February AWS/GCP Cross-Posting Error:** It appears the AWS and GCP amounts were swapped in QBO for February 2025. The AWS invoice shows $1,262.32 but QBO has $412.50, while GCP invoice shows $425.00 but QBO has $1,262.32. This looks like a data entry reversal.\n\n2. **Slack January Missing:** The Slack invoice for January ($150.00) was not recorded in QBO, which shows $0.00.\n\n3. **Microsoft 365 December Missing:** The Microsoft 365 invoice for December ($99.99) was not recorded in QBO, which shows $0.00.\n\n4. **AWS April Underpayment:** Small $50 discrepancy suggests either a partial payment or data entry error.\n\n---\n\n### 📁 **Deliverables**\n\nThe complete reconciliation has been saved to:\n- **CSV file:** `output/reconciliation_output.csv`\n\nThis file contains all 84 line items with:\n- Vendor name\n- Period\n- Invoice amount\n- QBO amount\n- Match status (Matched/Partial/Unmatched)\n- Detailed notes on each discrepancy\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 **Recommended Actions**\n\n1. **Investigate February swap:** Review AWS and GCP entries for February 2025 to confirm if amounts were reversed\n2. **Record missing Slack payment:** Add $150.00 Slack expense for January 2025\n3. **Record missing Microsoft 365 payment:** Add $99.99 Microsoft 365 expense for December 2025\n4. **Review AWS April:** Verify the $50 difference and determine if additional payment is owed or if invoice is incorrect\n\nWould you like me to create a styled Excel report with color-coded discrepancies for easier review?', 'type': 'text'}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How confident are you in your reconciliation results? Rate your confidence (0-100%) and identify any areas of uncertainty.'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929', 'system': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'JSON array of matched records. Each: {"vendor": "Comcast Business", "period": "Jan 2025", "invoice_amount": 189.00, "qbo_amount": 189.00}'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json']}}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'type': 'string', 'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv'}, 'output_filename': {'type': 'string', 'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx'}}, 'required': []}}]}}
